==Problem==
A square with side length <math>x</math> is inscribed in a right triangle with sides of length <math>3</math>, <math>4</math>, and <math>5</math> so that one vertex of the square coincides with the right-angle vertex of the triangle. A square with side length <math>y</math> is inscribed in another right triangle with sides of length <math>3</math>, <math>4</math>, and <math>5</math> so that one side of the square lies on the hypotenuse of the triangle. What is <math>\dfrac{x}{y}</math>?

<math>\textbf{(A) } \dfrac{12}{13} \qquad \textbf{(B) } \dfrac{35}{37} \qquad \textbf{(C) } 1 \qquad \textbf{(D) } \dfrac{37}{35} \qquad \textbf{(E) } \dfrac{13}{12}</math>

==Solution 1==

Analyze the first right triangle.

<asy>
pair A,B,C;
pair D, e, F;
A = (0,0);
B = (4,0);
C = (0,3);

D = (0, 12/7);
e = (12/7 , 12/7);
F = (12/7, 0);

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(D--e--F);

label("$x$", D/2, W);
label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, N);
label("$D$", D, W);
label("$E$", e, NE);
label("$F$", F, S);
</asy>

Note that <math>\triangle ABC</math> and <math>\triangle FBE</math> are similar, so <math>\frac{BF}{FE} = \frac{AB}{AC}</math>. This can be written as <math>\frac{4-x}{x}=\frac{4}{3}</math>. Solving, <math>x = \frac{12}{7}</math>.

Now we analyze the second triangle.


<asy>
pair A,B,C;
pair q, R, S, T;
A = (0,0);
B = (4,0);
C = (0,3);

q = (1.297, 0);
R = (2.27 , 1.297);
S = (0.973, 2.27);
T  = (0, 0.973);

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(q--R--S--T--cycle);

label("$y$", (q+R)/2, NW);
label("$A'$", A, SW);
label("$B'$", B, SE);
label("$C'$", C, N);
label("$Q$", (q-(0,0.3)));
label("$R$", R, NE);
label("$S$", S, NE);
label("$T$", T, W);
</asy>

Similarly, <math>\triangle A'B'C'</math> and <math>\triangle RB'Q</math> are similar, so <math>RB' = \frac{4}{3}y</math>, and <math>C'S = \frac{3}{4}y</math>. Thus, <math>C'B' = C'S + SR + RB' = \frac{4}{3}y + y + \frac{3}{4}y = 5</math>. Solving for <math>y</math>, we get <math>y = \frac{60}{37}</math>. Thus, <math>\frac{x}{y} = \boxed{\textbf{(D)}\:\frac{37}{35}}</math>.


==Solution 2 (Full Coordinate Bash for x and y)==

Set the right-angle vertex of the triangle as <imath>(0,0)</imath>. Notice that the hypotenuse of the triangle can be described by the line equation <imath>y = 3 - \frac{3}{4}x</imath>, which simplifies to <imath>3x + 4y - 12 = 0</imath>. 

To find <imath>x</imath>, the top-right vertex of the first square lies on this hypotenuse and can be described by the line <imath>y=x</imath>. Hence, we may solve for <imath>x</imath> by solving <imath>3x + 4x = 12</imath>, which yields <imath>x = \frac{12}{7}</imath>.

To find <imath>y</imath>, let the two corners of the second square that touch the sides of the triangle be <imath>Q(x_Q, 0)</imath> and <imath>T(0, y_T)</imath>. Pushing these points straight out by a distance of <imath>y</imath> gives the other two corners on the hypotenuse:
<cmath>R = \left(x_Q + \frac{3}{5}y, \frac{4}{5}y\right)</cmath>
<cmath>S = \left(\frac{3}{5}y, y_T + \frac{4}{5}y\right)</cmath>

Since <imath>R</imath> and <imath>S</imath> lie on the hypotenuse line <imath>3x + 4y = 12</imath>, we can plug them in to find <imath>x_Q</imath> and <imath>y_T</imath>:
<cmath>3\left(x_Q + \frac{3}{5}y\right) + 4\left(\frac{4}{5}y\right) = 12 \implies x_Q = \frac{12-5y}{3}</cmath>
<cmath>3\left(\frac{3}{5}y\right) + 4\left(y_T + \frac{4}{5}y\right) = 12 \implies y_T = \frac{12-5y}{4}</cmath>

The distance between the two points <imath>Q</imath> and <imath>T</imath> is just the side of the square <imath>y</imath>. Using the distance formula:
<cmath>\left(\frac{12-5y}{3}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{12-5y}{4}\right)^2 = y^2</cmath>
<cmath>(12-5y)^2 \left(\frac{1}{9} + \frac{1}{16}\right) = y^2</cmath>
<cmath>(12-5y)^2 \left(\frac{25}{144}\right) = y^2</cmath>

Taking the square root of both sides gives:
<cmath>\frac{5}{12}(12-5y) = y \implies 60 - 25y = 12y \implies 37y = 60 \implies y = \frac{60}{37}</cmath>

Thus, <imath>\frac{x}{y} = \frac{12/7}{60/37} = \boxed{\textbf{(D)}\:\frac{37}{35}}</imath>.

~viviviiccotrrr1098

==Solution 3 (Alternate solution in finding x)==
Set the right-angle vertex of the triangle as <imath>(0,0)</imath>. Notice that the hypotenuse of the triangle, as depicted in solution one, can be described by <imath>y = 3 - \frac{3}{4}x</imath>, while <imath>AE</imath> can be describe by <imath>y=x</imath>. Hence, we may solve for <imath>x</imath> by solving <imath> 3- \frac{3}{4}x = x</imath>, which yields <imath>\frac{12}{7}</imath>.

Proceed by finding the value of y via the method described in solution 1, and we will get <imath>y = \frac{60}{37}</imath>. Thus, <imath>\frac{x}{y} = \boxed{\textbf{(D)}\:\frac{37}{35}}</imath>.

==Note==
In general, if the legs were <math>a</math> and <math>b</math>, we have that 
<cmath>x= \frac{ab}{a+b}, y=\frac{ab\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}{a^2+ab+b^2}.</cmath> 
This can be verified by plugging in <math>a=3</math> and <math>b=4</math>.
